    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX2004",
        title: "enum variant payload arity mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S2",
        template: "enum variant `{enum_name}::{variant_name}` expects {expected} payload {plural}, got {actual}",
        explain: "An enum variant constructor or match pattern supplied a different number of positional payload components than the variant declaration. Under RFC-074 §2.2, enum variants carry a positional `typs[]` payload list (arity = `len(typs)`); arity 0 = unit variant, arity 1 = single-field, arity N≥2 = multi-field. The construction or pattern site must match the declared arity exactly. Fix: add or remove payload components, or amend the enum declaration.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    // RFC-074 Phase 2.1 (2026-05-20) — enum variant payload element-type check.
    // Stage S3 (types) emits this AFTER HX2004 has confirmed matching arity. For
    // each positional slot in `E::V(arg_0, arg_1, ..., arg_{N-1})` the inferred
    // type of arg_i must be assignable to the declared `typs[i]` from the enum
    // variant declaration. HX2004 (arity) and HX2005 (element-type) form the
    // matched pair gating the positive type-soundness obligation per RFC-074 §3
    // Phase 2 + Phase 2.1.
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX2005",
        title: "enum variant payload element-type mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "enum variant `{enum_name}::{variant_name}` payload slot {pos}: expected `{expected}`, got `{actual}`",
        explain: "An enum variant constructor supplied a payload value whose static type does not match the declared positional slot type. Under RFC-074 §2.2 each variant carries a `typs: [TypeRef]` list; the construction site's `children[i]` must be type-assignable to `typs[i]`. Common causes: argument-order swap (e.g. `E::V(\"hi\", 42)` against `enum E { V(int, string) }`), a typo in the enum declaration, or a stale call site after a variant signature change. Fix: reorder the arguments, correct the expression types, or amend the enum variant declaration.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    // #2663 (2026-06-04) — assignment to an immutable binding. Stage S2 (bind)
    // emits this when an assignment LHS (`x = …`, `x += …`, …) is a FUNCTION-
    // LOCAL Ident whose innermost binding is a non-`mut` `let` or non-`mut`
    // param. Before this check, the 0.1.0-dispatch codegen SILENTLY DROPPED a
    // write to such a slot — for a loop counter the slot the condition reads
    // never updated → infinite loop / hang, with NO compile-time diagnostic.
    //
    // Severity is WARNING by default (NOT Error): a scope sweep of the in-tree
    // corpus found ~1033 pre-existing non-`mut` local writes across 76 files
    // (crypto/aws-sigv4/math/atlas-verifiers) that the silent-drop tolerated
    // outside loops. Hard-erroring all of them would brick the self-host build
    // before stdlib is migrated, so HX2006 lands as a VISIBLE warning first —
    // turning the previously-SILENT hang into a diagnosable one — and escalates
    // to a hard Error under `HEXA_STRICT_LET=1` (see _emit_hx2006 in bind.hexa),
    // matching the existing self/type_checker.hexa strict-let gate. The
    // canonical `let mut` idiom is never flagged.
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX2006",
        title: "cannot assign to immutable binding",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S2",
        template: "cannot assign to immutable binding `{name}` (declared without `mut`)",
        explain: "The assignment target is a function-local `let` binding (or parameter) declared without `mut`, so it should not be reassigned. Under hexa 0.1.0-dispatch such a write was silently dropped by codegen — the binding kept its initial value, so a loop counter advanced with a non-`mut` `let` never terminated (#2663, anima #1795). The bind pass now flags the write at compile time. Default severity is a warning (the in-tree corpus still carries ~1033 such writes pending migration); set HEXA_STRICT_LET=1 to escalate to a hard error. Fix: declare the binding `let mut <name> = …` if it is meant to change, or remove the reassignment. Shadowing with a fresh `let <name> = …` in an inner scope is still allowed; only reassigning the SAME binding is flagged.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Replace
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3001",
        title: "type mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "type mismatch: expected `{expected}`, got `{actual}`",
        explain: "A binary operator, assignment, or other expression site received an operand whose static type does not match the expected type. Insert an explicit cast or fix the producing expression.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
