    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3010",
        title: "duplicate pub symbol across files",
        // Warning, not Error: the bind/typecheck pre-pass already
        // resolves the collision with a first-wins skip (so the
        // second-file copy is silently dropped from the typed env);
        // hard-erroring on top of that blocks codegen even when the
        // duplicates are byte-identical helpers re-exported from
        // multiple atlas modules. Downstream callers see exactly one
        // definition either way — this stays a warning surface so the
        // user notices the dup without losing the build.
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "duplicate `pub` definition of `{name}`: also defined in `{first_file}` (importer `{second_file}`)",
        explain: "Two distinct files both declare `pub <name>` and end up spliced into the same compilation unit. The pre-pass keeps the first definition encountered and skips the rest, so this is informational unless the two definitions actually disagree — in which case behaviour follows the first definition.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3005",
        title: "match arms have different types",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "match arms have different types: expected `{expected}`, got `{actual}` on arm {pos}",
        explain: "All arms of a `match` used as an expression must produce the same type. Adjust the divergent arm to match the first arm's result type.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3006",
        title: "non-exhaustive match",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "non-exhaustive match on enum `{enum}`: missing variant(s) {missing}; add the arm(s) or a `_` wildcard",
        explain: "A `match` over an enum should cover every variant. This warning implements the single-column case of Maranget's usefulness algorithm (Luc Maranget, \"Warnings for pattern matching\", JFP 2007): the set of head constructors covered by the (unguarded) arms is compared against the enum's full variant signature; any variant not covered AND not subsumed by a `_` wildcard or an irrefutable ident catch-all makes the wildcard row useful, i.e. the match is non-exhaustive. Guarded arms (`Variant if cond`) do NOT count toward coverage because the guard may fail at runtime. This is a non-fatal warning (S3) — codegen still emits a no-value fall-through for an unmatched scrutinee, so existing builds are unaffected. Fix: add the missing variant arms, or a final `_ -> …` wildcard if the omission is intentional.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3011",
        title: "mismatched types",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "mismatched types: expected `{expected}`, found `{actual}`",
        explain: "Opt-in static type check (`HEXA_STATIC_TYPES=1`). A local binding, assignment, or struct-literal field-init declared (or its place has) an explicit annotation `T` whose primitive type provably differs from the initialiser's inferred type — e.g. `let x: i32 = \"s\"` or `let ok: bool = 1`. This is the Rust E0308 'mismatched types' rule brought to LOCAL (in-fn-body) positions: a string/bool/char literal never coerces to a numeric annotation, and an integer literal never coerces to `bool`. The default build does NOT emit this (the flag is off → diagnostic stream byte-identical to today); under `HEXA_STATIC_TYPES=1` it is a WARNING by default (non-fatal, F4 warn-first two-step) — set `HEXA_STATIC_TYPES_STRICT=1` to escalate it to a build-refusing error that aborts before codegen (REJECT). Coverage is a scope-local rung set (all KNOWN-scalar-primitive vs KNOWN-scalar-primitive only): r1 scalar-literal RHS on a typed `let`; r2 ident/call RHS on a typed `let`; r3 ident-LHS re-assignment; r4 array-element assign `a[i] = …`; r5 struct-field assign `s.field = …`; r6 struct-literal field-init `Point{ x: … }`; r8b param/module-let array-base index-assign (with `HEXA_STATIC_TYPES_ARRAY_LOWER=1`); r9a Index/Field READ inference feeding the let/assign rungs (Index needs ARRAY_LOWER); r9c match literal-pattern vs scrutinee. Array element types flow structurally under `HEXA_STATIC_TYPES_ARRAY_LOWER=1` (r7-prep/r8/r9b), feeding the default-ON HX3003/HX3004 sites; struct-literal field EXISTENCE is HX3016 (r9d). Generic/trait static-dispatch and nested receivers (`s.inner.x`) are a measured ceiling — the `_infer_expr` scope cannot reach them (dynamic runtime type-erasure). Fix: change the value to match the annotation, or the annotation to match the value. The integer-literal/float-literal coercions Rust allows (`let x: i32 = 5`, `let y: f64 = 1.5`) still type-check.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3012",
        title: "use of moved value",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "use of moved value `{name}`: this `@own` binding (declared at line {def_line}) was already moved at line {moved_line}",
        explain: "Opt-in ownership lint (`HEXA_OWN_LINT=1`). `@own` marks a `let` binding as a SINGLE-OWNER value: the first time its bare name is consumed by a move site — the RHS of a later `let`, the RHS of an assignment, a call argument, or a bare-ident `return` value — ownership is considered transferred out of the binding. This warning fires on any SECOND read/move of the same binding after that transfer, mirroring Rust's E0382 'borrow of moved value' shape (rustc_borrowck use-after-move) as a non-fatal WARNING: the build still compiles and codegen is untouched — this is a lint band, not a REJECT. Scope is HEXA-OWN L2 r1+r2: block-local `@own let` bindings inside one fn body, including return position (`return x` warns when `x` was already moved, and itself counts as the move for any later use); fn params, struct fields, borrows (`&`), and drop insertion are later rungs. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing and populates no registry — the diagnostic stream is byte-identical (byteeq-neutral). Fix: don't reuse the binding after its move — restructure so each owned value has exactly one consumer, clone/recompute the value, or drop the `@own` annotation if the binding is intentionally shared.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3013",
        title: "arena-allocated value escapes to a module global",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "arena escape: fn-local composite `{name}` (declared at line {def_line}) is stored into module global `{global}`; the fn arena is reclaimed on return and only the RETURN value is heapified",
        explain: "Opt-in arena-escape lint (`HEXA_ARENA_ESCAPE_LINT=1`), HEXA-OWN L2.5 r1 Rule B. On the default fn-arena path (hexa_val_arena_on) a composite literal built inside a fn — an array literal `[…]` or struct literal `S{…}` — is bump-allocated in that fn's arena scope. `__hexa_fn_arena_return` heapifies ONLY the fn's RETURN value before `scope_pop` reclaims the arena, and `hexa_array_push` dynamically heapifies push-into-outer-array — but a store into a MODULE-LEVEL `let` global is the runtime-admitted unheapified escape (self/runtime_core.c:3432): after the fn returns, the global references reclaimed arena memory. This lint warns when a fn-local `let` binding whose initialiser was a composite literal is later assigned (bare-ident RHS) to a module-level `let` global not shadowed by a fn-local binding. Non-fatal WARNING band (build still compiles; codegen/runtime untouched — check-side only, native aprime frontend). Scope is L2.5 r1: direct `global = local_composite` assignment inside one fn body; gen2 `@no_arena` contract-violation scanning (Rule A), stores through fields/indices, closure captures, and region-returns depth are later rungs. The default build (flag unset) emits nothing and populates no registry — the diagnostic stream is byte-identical (byteeq-neutral). Fix: return the composite and assign at the caller, build the global's value at module scope, or copy element-by-element into a heap-resident structure instead of aliasing the fn-arena allocation.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3014",
        title: "shared-handle aliasing hazard",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "shared-handle hazard ({rule}): `{name}` is used after `{other}` at line {line} in the same block; both names refer to the same underlying value",
        explain: "Opt-in MIR intra-block loan pass (`HEXA_BORROWCK=1`), HEXA-OWN L3 M3. hexa aggregates (arrays, map-backed structs) are REFERENCE values: `let b = a` copies the handle, not the buffer, so a write through one name is visible through every alias. This warning fires on two intra-block shapes, both at HIR→MIR lowering time (native aprime frontend; spans in hand): Rule 1 'aliased write' — a value is used through one name after the SAME underlying value was written through a DIFFERENT name (field-assign `x.f = v`, index-assign `a[i] = v`, or a receiver-mutating method `a.push/pop/truncate/set`) earlier in the same straight-line block — the mutate-one-alias-then-read-the-other shape that silently observes the mutation; benign read-only aliasing (`let b = a` + reads through both) never fires. Rule 2 'use after move' — a binding whose value was passed into a callee parameter declared `@own` (ownership transfer) is used again in the same block; call args into non-`@own` params are READS, never moves, so ordinary calls never fire. Scope is deliberately intra-block (every if/while/match/for splits blocks — cross-branch flows are the L3 M4 dataflow rung) and whole-local (no place projections in MIR Operands: a write to `x.a` also arms reads of `x.b`). Aliases created via fn returns, struct-field/array-element loads, cross-param aliasing, and unknown-typed locals are not tracked (precision-first: quiet false-negatives over loud false-positives). Non-fatal WARNING band: the build compiles and codegen is untouched — the pass only observes the lowering; the default build (flag unset) populates no registry and the diagnostic stream plus emitted binary are byte-identical (byteeq-neutral). Fix: don't interleave a write through one alias with uses through another in the same block — mutate through a single name, copy the aggregate element-by-element if independent buffers were intended, or restructure so the moved-from binding is not reused.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3016",
        title: "struct has no field with this name",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "struct `{name}` has no field named `{field}`",
        explain: "Opt-in static type check (`HEXA_STATIC_TYPES=1`), static-types r9d. A struct-literal field-init names a field the struct does not declare — e.g. `Point{ x: 1, z: 2 }` when `Point` declares only `x`/`y`. This is the Rust E0560 'struct has no field named' rule (rustc_hir_typeck check_expr_struct_fields → report_unknown_field), and it also hardens the r6 field-init TYPE check: before r9d a typo'd field name silently skipped both the existence and the type comparison. The check reads the same (struct,field) side registry r5/r6 use; a struct name the registry does not know (cross-module or unresolved receiver) stays SILENT — conservative unknown-fallback, consistent with the ladder's under-reject contract. Missing REQUIRED fields (Rust E0063) are deliberately NOT this diagnostic — that is a separate warning-band follow-on. The default build does NOT emit this (flag off → diagnostic stream byte-identical); under `HEXA_STATIC_TYPES=1` it is a WARNING by default (F4 warn-first two-step), and `HEXA_STATIC_TYPES_STRICT=1` escalates it to a build-refusing error (REJECT), the same strict abort contract as HX3011. Fix: correct the field name, or add the field to the struct declaration.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3017",
        title: "struct literal is missing a required field",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "struct `{name}` is missing field `{field}`",
        explain: "Opt-in static type check (`HEXA_STATIC_TYPES=1`), static-types r11. A struct-literal OMITS a field the struct DECLARES — e.g. `Point{ x: 1 }` when `Point` declares both `x` AND `y`. This is the Rust E0063 'missing field in initializer' rule (rustc_hir_typeck check_expr_struct_fields → report_missing_fields), the PAIR of HX3016 (E0560, a PRESENT field the struct does not declare): HX3016 fires when a provided field is unknown, HX3017 when a declared field is absent. The check reads the same (struct,field) side registry r5/r6/r9d use; it computes (declared_fields − provided_fields) and emits one HX3017 per missing declared field. A struct name the registry does not know (cross-module or unresolved receiver) stays SILENT — the same conservative unknown-fallback as HX3016. UNLIKE HX3011/HX3016 this is a NON-FATAL WARNING (band, not REJECT): the build still compiles and codegen is untouched — SSOT F4 warn-first, so a partially-initialised literal is surfaced without aborting. The default build does NOT emit this (flag off → no registry populated, no diagnostic, emitted binary + diagnostic stream byte-identical to today). Fix: add the missing field(s) to the literal, or give the struct a default so the omission is intentional.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX4001",
        title: "integer division domain narrow",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S4",
        template: "integer division narrows the real domain to integers; use `//` (floor) or cast to f64 to silence",
        explain: "Dividing two integer operands with `/` silently truncates the real-number result. RFC-017 S4 surfaces this as a domain hint so the author confirms the narrowing was intentional.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX5001",
        title: "unit mismatch in arithmetic",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S5",
        template: "unit mismatch in `{op}`: lhs is `{lhs}`, rhs is `{rhs}`",
        explain: "Addition, subtraction, and comparison operators require both sides to share the same dimensional unit (RFC-017 §3.1 S5). Multiplication and division compose units; only +/-/comparisons require equality. Lenient v1: an untyped operand is compatible with any unit.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX5002",
        title: "fn return unit mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S5",
        template: "return unit mismatch: function `{name}` declares `{expected}`, body produces `{actual}`",
        explain: "A `return` expression (or a fn's trailing block value) yields a value whose dimensional unit does not match the unit declared in the fn's `@units` annotation.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX5003",
        title: "call argument unit mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S5",
        template: "argument {pos} of `{name}`: expected unit `{expected}`, got `{actual}`",
        explain: "A call argument's dimensional unit does not match the corresponding parameter unit declared on the callee's `@units` annotation. Convert the argument or fix the callee declaration.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX6001",
        title: "equational normalization mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S6",
        template: "@verify equational mismatch in `{name}`: LHS normalizes to `{lhs}`, RHS normalizes to `{rhs}`",
        explain: "The `@verify` annotation requested an equational check, but after constant folding, commutative operand sorting, and associativity flattening the LHS and RHS of the body's `==` comparison reduce to different canonical forms. RFC-017 §3.1 S6 — the in-house prover's first-line equational rewrite engine cannot prove the stated equality syntactically. Fix the body to express a true identity, or fall back to `@prove` for SMT-class reasoning.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX6002",
        title: "sample-eval counter-example found",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S6",
        template: "@verify counter-example in `{name}`: at {assignment} LHS evaluates to `{lhs}` but RHS evaluates to `{rhs}`",
        explain: "The sample-eval prover stage exercised the body's `==` comparison at 16 deterministic integer assignments (LCG seed=1, range [-10, 10]) and found at least one input where LHS and RHS disagree. The reported assignment is a concrete witness — the equality cannot hold over Int. Refine the body, narrow domain via `@units`, or upgrade to `@prove` for SMT-grade verification.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8001",
        title: "atlas L node missing for @implements",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "atlas {atlas_hash} has no L node `{node_id}` cited by @implements — fix the citation id, or if L[{node_id}] should exist, register it: `@discover(kind=\"L\")` (upstream: `hexa atlas register <file>` → `promote` if you own hexa-lang **||** `pr` otherwise).",
        explain: "An `@implements(L[id])` annotation refers to an L node that is not present in the merged atlas snapshot. Either fix the citation id (typo / wrong node), or — if the law genuinely belongs in the atlas — register it with `@discover(kind=\"L\")` and land it via the `hexa atlas` CLI: `hexa atlas register <file.hexa>` writes the proposed shard; `hexa atlas promote --staging <shard>` folds it into the live atlas (the `update` arm — repo owners; emits atlas.append.<date>.n6); or `hexa atlas pr` branches + `gh pr create`s against origin (the `PR` arm — non-owners).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Replace
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8002",
        title: "@implements points at non-L node kind",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "@implements({kind}[{node_id}]) points at a {kind} node; only L nodes may be implemented",
        explain: "The `@implements` annotation is reserved for citing an L (law) node from the atlas. Citing a P/C/E node is a kind error — use the appropriate annotation or cite the law that governs the node.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8003",
        title: "fn uses atlas L refs but has no @implements citation",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "function `{name}` references atlas L node `{node_id}` in its body but has no @implements citation",
        explain: "A function body cites one or more L nodes (via L[id] expressions) but the function declares no `@implements` annotation. Adding `@implements(L[id])` records the law-of-record so downstream verification (S6/S7 prover) can attach proofs to the right node. DEPRECATED: superseded by HX8004 (Error) per SPEC.yaml atlas_citation_strict; kept transitionally for tooling parity. Will be retired in the next citation pass refresh.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Insert
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8004",
        title: "formula has no atlas binding",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "formula in `{name}` has no atlas binding — `@implements(L[id])` to cite an existing law, or `@discover(kind=\"L\")` to register a new one (upstream: `hexa atlas register <file>` → `promote` if you own hexa-lang **||** `pr` otherwise). Bypass: `@grace(HX8004, until=\"...\", reason=\"...\")` — never silent (emits HX9000). SPEC.yaml atlas_citation_strict.",
        explain: "Functions carrying @verify or @law annotations, OR whose body references atlas L[*] nodes, are knowledge-bearing artifacts — they must be bound to atlas L. Cite an existing law with `@implements(L[id])`, or register a new one with `@discover(kind=\"L\")`. Land it upstream via the `hexa atlas` CLI: `hexa atlas register <file.hexa>` writes the proposed shard (compiler/discover/staging.hexa); then `hexa atlas promote --staging <shard>` folds it into the live atlas if you own hexa-lang (the `update` arm — emits atlas.append.<date>.n6, so the atlas grows with every contribution); or `hexa atlas pr` branches + `gh pr create`s against origin (the `PR` arm — non-owners). Bypass while a migration is in flight: `@grace(HX8004, until, reason)` — never silently (every @grace site emits HX9000 and the build is gated on acked-grace consent).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::Insert
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX9000",
        title: "@grace requires reviewer acknowledgement",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "runtime",
        template: "ai-native: @grace at {file}:{line} suppresses {error_code} until {until}. Reason: \"{reason}\". Suppressed [{suppressed_severity_original}]: {suppressed_title} — {suppressed_message}. This is a TEMPORARY debt — the author is responsible for resolving the underlying issue before the expiry date. Reviewer acknowledgement REQUIRED before merge.",
        explain: "Every @grace site emits this warning to prevent silent strict-lint bypass. The build is gated on explicit user consent at the CI/PR layer: PR description must contain `Acked-grace: HXxxxx by <reviewer>` trailer or commit message must contain `acked-grace: <code> by <author>`. The `Suppressed [...]` segment inlines the original diagnostic's severity / title / short-rendered body (SPEC.yaml opt_out.ai_native_warn_policy.suppressed_diagnostic_inlining, args_keys: suppressed_title / suppressed_message / suppressed_severity_original) so the bypassed text stays auditable at every compile. SPEC.yaml opt_out.ai_native_warn_policy.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX9001",
        title: "@grace expired",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "runtime",
        template: "@grace for {error_code} expired on {until}; fix or extend",
        explain: "An @grace annotation past its `until` date no longer suppresses the underlying diagnostic. Either fix the issue or update `until` (and `reason`).",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX8010",
        title: "@law annotation malformed",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "@law annotation malformed: {detail}",
        explain: "The `@law` annotation must take at least one positional argument: a string literal containing the formula (e.g. `@law(\"E = m * c^2\")`). Optional keyword args such as `units=\"...\"` are also string literals. RFC-017 §3.5.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX9002",
        title: "@grace annotation malformed",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "@grace annotation malformed: {detail}",
        explain: "The `@grace` annotation requires three fields: a positional HX error code (matching `HX[0-9]{4}`), `until=\"YYYY-MM-DD\"`, and `reason=\"...\"`. SPEC.yaml opt_out section.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX9003",
        title: "@discover annotation malformed",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S8",
        template: "@discover annotation malformed: {detail}",
        explain: "The `@discover` annotation requires a `kind=` keyword argument whose value is one of the atlas node-kind strings: `\"P\"`, `\"C\"`, `\"L\"`, or `\"E\"`.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    // ── RFC 055 055-P1 — `@gpu` subset strict-lint (gpu/SPEC.md §9) ──
    //
    // These codes classify a `@gpu_kernel` / `@gpu_device` function
    // against the gpu/SPEC.md subset. The decision table lives in
    // `compiler/codegen/nvptx_target.hexa::nvptx_validate_gpu_subset`;
    // wiring it into the strict-lint pipeline (FnDecl walker + diag
    // emit) is a 055-P2 deliverable. Today the codes are pre-registered
    // here so a future PR plumbs them in without growing the catalog.
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU01",
        title: "@gpu_kernel must return void",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "@gpu_kernel `{fn_name}` declares a non-void return type ({type_name}); PTX `.visible .entry` cannot return a value",
        explain: "A `@gpu_kernel` lowers to a PTX `.visible .entry`, which has no return-value channel — results must go through a `.global` farr the host reads back. Either change the return to void and write the result through an output `[f64]` parameter, or convert the function to `@gpu_device` (which lowers to `.func` and may return a value). gpu/SPEC.md §2 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU02",
        title: "@gpu_device called from CPU code",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "@gpu_device `{fn_name}` called from CPU code; @gpu_device functions are reachable only from @gpu_kernel / @gpu_device bodies",
        explain: "A `@gpu_device` function lowers to PTX `.func` — it lives in the kernel's address space and cannot be invoked from the host. To call it from CPU code, either route through a `@gpu_kernel` entry point or copy the body to a regular CPU `fn`. gpu/SPEC.md §2 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU03",
        title: "CPU fn called from @gpu_* body",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "CPU fn `{callee}` called from @gpu_* body `{fn_name}`; only allowlisted intrinsics and @gpu_device fns are callable from GPU code",
        explain: "A GPU kernel runs on the device — host-side functions are not in its address space. The legal call surface inside a `@gpu_*` body is: (1) the gpu_* intrinsics (gpu/SPEC.md §5), and (2) other `@gpu_device` functions. Move the helper to `@gpu_device` or inline the body. gpu/SPEC.md §4 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU04",
        title: "non-allowlisted type in @gpu_* function",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "type `{type_name}` is not in the @gpu subset allowlist; legal types are f64 / f32 / i64 / i32 / bool and fixed/borrowed arrays of those",
        explain: "A GPU kernel has no host heap and no allocator — only fixed-extent scalar + array types are legal. Strings, maps, sets, growable Lists, tuples of non-allowlisted types, closures, and trait objects all reject. Use a `[f64]` / `[i64]` farr instead. gpu/SPEC.md §3 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU05",
        title: "denied construct in @gpu_* body",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "@gpu_* body contains a denied construct ({detail}); recursion / heap alloc / growable-collection op / IO / syscall are all rejected",
        explain: "A GPU kernel has no growable call stack, no host heap, and no syscall surface. Rewrite the construct as a fixed-extent computation: convert recursion to iteration, replace growing collections with caller-provided `[T]` farrs, and remove IO calls (a kernel cannot println / read files). gpu/SPEC.md §4 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU05W",
        title: "@gpu_* loop with non-obviously-bounded iteration count",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "@gpu_* loop in `{fn_name}` has an iteration count the validator cannot prove terminates; rewrite as a thread-indexed loop with compile-visible bounds",
        explain: "A GPU kernel must terminate — unbounded loops can stall an entire SM. The validator accepts `for i in 0..N` with `N` a constant or kernel parameter; loops keyed off a `while` over a runtime-mutated index are warned. gpu/SPEC.md §4 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU06",
        title: "gpu_* intrinsic in CPU code",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "intrinsic `{intrinsic}` is only legal inside a @gpu_* body; calling it from CPU code is a GPU/CPU phase-confusion error",
        explain: "Intrinsics like `gpu_thread_id_x` / `gpu_block_dim_x` / `gpu_barrier` lower to PTX special-register reads and only have meaning on the device. Move the call into a `@gpu_kernel` body, or compute the value host-side from the launch configuration. gpu/SPEC.md §5 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "GPU07",
        title: "@shared misuse",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "codegen",
        template: "@shared on `{local_name}` is illegal here; @shared is only legal on a fixed-extent local array inside a @gpu_kernel body",
        explain: "Shared memory is block-scoped and exists only inside a kernel launch. `@shared` must annotate a local of fixed-extent array type (`[T; N]`) of an allowlisted scalar, declared inside a `@gpu_kernel` (not a `@gpu_device`). gpu/SPEC.md §6 / §9.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3007",
        title: "const generic argument kind mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "const generic mismatch on `{owner}`: {detail}",
        explain: "A const generic parameter (`struct Buf<const N> { … }`) is instantiated with a *value* and a type parameter (`struct Box<T> { … }`) with a *type*; the two slots are not interchangeable (Rust RFC 2000 min_const_generics — `Buf<int>` is rejected with \"expected a const but found a type\", and `Box<5>` with the inverse). The check decodes the synthetic `__cg` annotation that the const-generics parser capture (opt-in `HEXA_CONST_GENERICS=1`) attaches at the declaration site (param kinds) and the instantiation site (argument kinds), then matches each positional slot. This diagnostic is emitted only when the opt-in const-generics pass is enabled; the default build is byte-identical.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3008",
        title: "trait bound not satisfied",
        // Warning by default (release integrity): introducing a new
        // diagnostic ships opt-in, so a violated bound surfaces as a
        // non-fatal warning. The opt-in `HEXA_STRICT_BOUNDS=1` flag
        // promotes the SAME site to a fatal error (emitted as HX3009).
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "the trait `{trait}` is not implemented for `{type}` (required by `{owner}`)",
        explain: "A generic parameter with a trait bound (`fn max_of<T: Ord>(…)` or the equivalent where-clause desugaring `fn f<T>(…) where T: Ord`, The Rust Reference §\"Where clauses\") must be instantiated with a type that satisfies the bound. The const-generics pass (opt-in `HEXA_CONST_GENERICS=1`) proves each `T: Bound` against a rustc-faithful builtin trait-impl table: the int family is `Ord`/`Eq`/`Num`/`Copy`/…, but the float family is `PartialOrd`/`PartialEq`/`Num`/… and NOT `Ord`/`Eq` (the NaN total-order hole — `f64: Ord` does not hold in Rust either). A bound joined with `+` (e.g. `T: Ord+Num`) emits one diagnostic per unsatisfied trait. Default severity is a warning; `HEXA_STRICT_BOUNDS=1` re-emits the same violation as the fatal HX3009.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        code: "HX3009",
        title: "trait bound not satisfied (strict)",
        severity: Severity::Error,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "the trait `{trait}` is not implemented for `{type}` (required by `{owner}`)",
        explain: "The strict-mode (`HEXA_STRICT_BOUNDS=1`) escalation of HX3008: a generic trait bound is provably unsatisfied at an instantiation site and the user has opted into fatal bound checking. See HX3008 for the bound-satisfaction model (rustc-faithful builtin impls; The Rust Reference §\"Where clauses\"). Emitted only under the opt-in const-generics pass; the default build is byte-identical.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    },
    DiagSpec {
        // Renumbered from HX3011 (static-types F0 pre-flip hygiene): the code
        // was double-booked with the "mismatched types" Error spec above —
        // `_catalog_lookup`-style first-match consumers and `hexa explain`
        // can only surface ONE spec per code, and a default-ON flip of the
        // static-types REJECT must own HX3011 unambiguously. Opt-in pass
        // (HEXA_HKT=1) → renumbering changes no default-build output.
        code: "HX3015",
        title: "higher-kinded type parameter arity mismatch",
        severity: Severity::Warning,
        stage: "S3",
        template: "type parameter `{param}` is used at conflicting kind-arities ({first_arity} vs {second_arity}) in `{owner}`",
        explain: "The opt-in higher-kinded-types pass (`HEXA_HKT=1`) checks kind-arity consistency: within one signature a declared type parameter must be used at a single kind. A parameter applied as `F<A>` is a unary type constructor (Haskell kind `*->*`, Scala `F[_]`); the same `F` may not also appear applied to a different number of arguments (`F<A,B>`) nor bare in a proper-type slot (kind `*`). This reproduces the core of Haskell kind inference (Haskell 2010 Report §4.1.1 — `f a` requires `f :: k->*` and `a :: k`; a mismatch is reported as \"Expected kind X, but has kind Y\") and Scala type-constructor polymorphism (Moors et al., \"Generics of a Higher Kind\", OOPSLA'08). CEILING (honest): this is ONLY the kind-arity fragment — full HKT (Functor/Monad typeclass polymorphism, instance resolution, type-level constructor application/`fmap` dispatch) is NOT implementable here, the same reason Rust RFC 1598 declines full `F: *->*` and offers generic associated types as a substitute: hexa has no trait/typeclass/instance registry, `class`/`instance`/kind-annotation syntax would violate the frozen seed, and the dynamic {tag,payload} runtime erases all generics to one monomorphic MIR (zero runtime realization). Default severity is a non-fatal warning; emitted only under the opt-in pass, so the default build is byte-identical.",
        fix_it_kind: FixItKind::None
    }
]
